  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic overview of common structural features and defects in a polycrystalline crystal. A regular lattice of two atom types forms the background, and highlighted boxes show how the ideal arrangement can be disrupted at different scales.\n\nIt starts from a single-crystal domain, where atoms are arranged in an ordered repeating pattern. Other highlighted regions show point defects, including a substituted atom, a missing atom vacancy, and an interstitial atom squeezed between normal lattice sites. Line defects are represented by a dislocation, with labels indicating edge and screw components, showing how an extra distortion in the lattice shifts neighboring atomic rows.\n\nAt larger scales, the image emphasizes interfaces and broken ordering. A grain boundary separates regions with different lattice orientation, while a stacking fault shows altered layer sequences, marked by local changes between ABA and ABC stacking. A void is shown as an empty region where atoms are absent altogether. Another inset depicts an inclusion or precipitate, where a small embedded phase has a different atomic pattern from the surrounding crystal.\n\nOverall, the figure summarizes how real polycrystalline materials deviate from perfect periodic order through multiple kinds of defects and internal boundaries.",
